00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.320
is that they have to simplify,
they have to sanitize,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.330
and they have to optimize
their infrastructure.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.746
And that's what I'm going to
talk to you guys about today.

00:03:20.746 --> 00:03:22.860
It's a very quick talk
from me, 10 minutes,

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.870
and I'll do my pitch.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.549
And Robin is going to come
and talk about their journey.

00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:31.090
Hopefully this is clear enough
for you guys to see from here.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.120
But this is
typically-- if I have

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.950
to summarize all the
architecture that I

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.680
see from my customers,
everyone that I talk to,

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.360
this is a general summarization
of what customers do.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.860
And I'm using Google products
here because I love the Google

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.484
products, and it's
a Google conference,

00:03:45.484 --> 00:03:48.585
you can replace these components
with open source solutions.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.220
So Pub/Sub, you can replace that
with Kafka, or Apache Kafka,

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.990
or any other sort
of ingest tool.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.540
Dataflow, Spark, Flink, they're
all open source solutions.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.480
BigQuery, data
warehousing solution,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.300
and you can replace that with
anything else that you like.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.160
But the general theme
is that customers

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.960
use some sort of real
time data ingest tool

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.630
like Pop/Sub or Kafka.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.430
They use some sort
of distributed system

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.560
to take that data and
put it into some sort

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.740
of data warehouse, some other
NoSQL databases, or something

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:20.760
else.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.420
What happens is that this is
sort of a good architecture,

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.144
but it's semi-optimized.

00:04:26.144 --> 00:04:27.810
And I don't know if
you guys-- actually,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.820
it's obvious what's
not optimized here--

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.000
you go from streaming,
you're doing streaming now.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.370
But towards the end
they start doing

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.320
some sort of
cleaning, aggregation,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.140
joining, and doing
discovery of data.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.670
All this stuff in red
takes time, takes a day.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.400
They wait for it to aggregate,
they wait for it to join data.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.840
Joining data takes a long time.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.039
So from that perspective,
this is not a really streaming

00:04:53.039 --> 00:04:53.580
architecture.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:57.420
This is streaming
at ingest, but still

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.130
baffling in a batch paradigm.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:01.650
So throughout this
talk, I'm just

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.350
going to unpack that, talk about
a few things that you guys'

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.120
customers should be able to do.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.197
Very high level, but hopefully
it's helpful to you guys.

00:05:10.197 --> 00:05:12.030
One of the first things
I want to talk about

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.500
is simplify your architecture.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.900
As much as you can, use simple
tools and simple platforms.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.750
It's tempting and
it's very valid

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.830
to use Spark, Dataflow,
Apache Beam, Flink,

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.480
all the different
distributed systems,

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.770
but it's common that I talk
to customers where all they do

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.990
is they take data
and do some cleaning.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.780
They do some sanitization.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.880
And if that's all
what you're doing,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.040
you don't need to use
a distributed system.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.260
It's OK to use it, but
with distributed system,

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.110
you have to maintain it, you
have to make sure it's updated,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.220
you have to make sure
the bugs are fixed.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.250
You have to deal with
semantics of exactly once

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.194
delivery, at least
once delivery,

00:05:52.194 --> 00:05:53.610
there's a lot of
complicated stuff

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.050
that you have to deal with.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.155
But if all you're
doing is simple tasks,

00:05:57.155 --> 00:05:58.530
it would be easy
to use something

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.000
like Cloud Functions--

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.160
that is, event
driven architecture.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.340
Data comes in, event gets
fired to Cloud Functions.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.720
You do something with it, and
put that data in destination,

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.130
and in the sink, or
wherever it needs to go.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.450
It's simple, it's server-less.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.050
You don't have to manage
an infrastructure,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.425
you don't have to
patch anything,

00:06:15.425 --> 00:06:17.610
you don't have to
DevOps or anything else.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.200
It's already taken
care of for you.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.110
So very simple, use
simple point of solutions,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.680
something like Cloud
Functions, or other things.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.090
Sanitize your data early.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.130
Generally, or very
common, what I see

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.275
is customers do cleaning of data
when data gets to destination.

00:06:33.275 --> 00:06:34.650
That's fine, and
it's much better

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.810
than many other
architecture I've seen,

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.550
but if what you're
doing is cleaning,

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.590
try to do cleaning
at ingest time.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:44.030
Try to do cleaning of data
at the Cloud Functions.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.110
If using Spark
streaming, at Spark,

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:50.660
Flink, Apache Beam
data flow, whatever.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.650
Do that cleaning
early on in a process.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.191
Most customers actually try to
do this, and they're doing it.

00:06:55.191 --> 00:06:59.650
So I think this point
should be simple to do.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.530
The other thing is
schema validation.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:04.050
What customers do is they
put data in destination

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.710
and they do discovery
to find data.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.900
But what I like my
customers to do,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.710
is they do schema validation
and schema registration much

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.202
earlier in the process.

00:07:15.202 --> 00:07:17.160
Because what happens is
that customers put data

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:18.030
in the destination.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.730
In this case, BigQuery actually
does the schema validation

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:21.377
for you.

00:07:21.377 --> 00:07:23.460
So you're in a good spot
if you're using BigQuery.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.190
But if your destination
or your sync

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:28.650
does not do schema
validation, make

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.800
sure that you do that early
on in the stage of a process.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.760
It helps you with
cleaning of data.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.621
It helps you with the
discovery of data.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.120
It helps you with
the documentation,

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:38.286
what's actually in the data.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:44.540
Oh, join.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:48.860
I would say eight out of 10
of slowness of any distributed

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.170
data system that I've
worked with with customers

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.180
comes from joining data sets.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:54.940
Joining data sets
that are big, joining

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.480
data sets that are
skewed, and joining data

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:01.470
sets that don't need to be
joined at the destination.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.100
So what I found out, and
what some of my customers

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:05.930
do, and I'd love
for you guys to do

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:09.580
is, if you think you
need to join common data

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:12.500
sets at the destination,
at the sink,

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.390
do that joining during
the ingest time.

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:18.320
For example, convert
your joints to look-ups.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.590
In this case, I'm using big
table as a NoSQL database

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:25.000
to do a look-up of
data as data comes in

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:28.250
and nest that data before
data gets sent to BigQuery.

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:30.710
So now I don't have to do
joining at the destination

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:31.250
level.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:33.685
It's not applicable
to every use case.

00:08:33.685 --> 00:08:35.059
I'm not saying
you can completely

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:36.990
get rid of join at destination.

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:40.700
But if you can at least 30,
40, 50% of your workloads

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:42.799
using nesting data set
you see an extremely

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:45.410
amount of good optimization
in your queries

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.000
and whatever you're
doing at the destination.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.760
So again, the pattern here is to
convert your joins to nesting.

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:58.290
Another thing is
pre-aggregation.

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.070
Another thing that
customers do often

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.920
is do aggregation at
the destination level.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.860
So they let data go in,
they'd write group bys,

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:10.600
and they'd do some
sort of aggregation.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.060
If you can't, very
similar to nesting

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:17.860
of data, as data comes in
from your ingest point,

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:20.890
try to do pre-aggregation
before data goes in.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.300
I've seen a blog--

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:26.350
one of the customers, what they
were doing is taking the data,

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:29.860
and as data comes in, they
keep a counter in the NoSQL

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.080
database, and they
use that counter

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.860
to do pre-aggregation of data.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.580
So for example, if they're doing
aggregation on a daily basis--

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.754
and let's just say
number of users per day--

00:09:38.754 --> 00:09:41.170
instead of waiting for that
data to get to the destination

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:45.460
and write the group by query
on top of that data sets,

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:49.210
what they do is they say
"Here's a simple look-up table

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:50.410
and big table.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.480
This is my day,
and here's a number

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.980
of counts for that
element in a given day."

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.660
And what they do is they put
that data in a big table.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.310
And interesting
thing is BigQuery

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:03.770
gives the ability to query
big table without moving data.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.920
So now, you have the
ability to join data

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.836
outside of your destination
that is already joined.

00:10:08.836 --> 00:10:10.960
Now you can write a query
that reads from big table

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:13.675
directly without actually
applying any group by.

00:10:13.675 --> 00:10:16.180
It simplifies your architecture,
simplifies your query,

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:21.730
and actually optimize and gives
you much better performance.

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:24.010
By far something that
customers are not doing today

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:28.060
and I'd love to see them do
is create a feedback loop.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:30.580
And feedback loop
is for you guys

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:34.990
to see what your system is
doing and keep improving it

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:36.490
as you see more workloads.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:40.030
And this is one of the best
practice of building a system,

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:41.560
like a complex system.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.210
Complex systems always have
sort of feedback loops.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.150
So as you're building
your infrastructure,

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:48.734
as you're building your
analytical infrastructure,

00:10:48.734 --> 00:10:49.900
use some sort of audit logs.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:51.733
For example, BigQuery
gives you an audit log

00:10:51.733 --> 00:10:54.730
as an extremely rich set
of data set that tells you

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:56.410
what BigQuery has
done, what queries

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.050
has been written, how
much bytes scanned,

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:01.390
what the cost of that query was.

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:03.960
So use that feedback
loop for using the audit

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.220
logs to understand what
queries have you run

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:08.030
for the last couple of weeks.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:09.010
Is it mostly group by?

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:09.890
Is it join?

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:11.830
Do you have a ton
of joins happening?

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.870
And run that report
on a weekly basis.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:16.930
And once you find these
repeated group bys

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:19.480
and joins, take
them apart and see

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.330
if you can implement that early
on in the stage of a streaming.

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:23.799
For example, Cloud Functions.

00:11:23.799 --> 00:11:25.840
Like I mentioned, bring
nesting into the picture,

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.460
bring pre-aggregation at the
ingest time into the picture.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:32.110
And run this report
every week just

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.959
to make sure you're optimizing
your infrastructure.

00:11:34.959 --> 00:11:37.000
And hopefully towards the
end this is the picture

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.700
you're going to have, where
data comes in from ingest point,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:42.910
you're doing pre-aggregation,
you're doing joins--

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.910
I'm sorry, you're doing
nesting instead of doing join--

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:47.290
at the ingest points.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:49.790
Clean data will go to
BigQuery or any destination

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:50.860
you guys have.

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:52.720
And you are writing
a federated queries

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:57.160
over big table, where data
is already aggregated.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:02.020
And now you have this end to end
