our sizes to lean more towards
from the cost efficiency

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:58.750
point of view.

00:25:58.750 --> 00:26:03.000
So this is a brief history how
our data science computational

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.760
infrastructure evolution path.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.130
So when I got here
in 2011, we only

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:11.100
have five note of Hadoop
we put it into AWS.

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:12.640
It was insanely expensive.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:15.970
You cannot believe how expensive
it is back to seven years ago

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:18.960
if you want to run five-node
tiny cluster in a cloud.

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:21.030
So the next year we
decide, OK, so we

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:25.104
want to run our own bare metal.

00:26:25.104 --> 00:26:26.520
We use a company
called Softlayer,

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:29.280
later on being acquired by IBM.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:31.410
And then in 2014,
we decide that's it,

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:35.040
we want to put x million
dollars, buy our own hardware,

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:36.630
and put it into a data center.

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:40.170
We actually put it a few
blocks away from our--

00:26:40.170 --> 00:26:44.220
actually the AWS US East 1
data center a few blocks away.

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:47.850
We tunnel them through with
a direction fiber link.

00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:50.730
And then we outsource all of
our data center operations

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:51.810
to Equinix.

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:54.120
We use level 3 as the network.

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:58.830
We were hire Metacloud to do
the virtualization layer for us.

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:00.750
We do all of these
outsources, but we still

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:02.460
got all of these
technical burdens,

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:06.180
like turnaround time of the
smart hands in a cage trying

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:10.560
to swap a node, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:16.620
So in 2017 we decided
to try Google Cloud.

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:19.890
And those are the considerations
again, scales and cost

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:23.220
and technicals are very
straightforward and intuitive.

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:26.970
So there is no upfront
capital anymore.

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:31.590
You don't need to worry about
hardware depreciation cost

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:36.450
and the piece that made us
to lean more towards Google

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:38.430
is actually our data strategy.

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:46.500
We have running in AWS for our
service layer for a long time.

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:50.160
But I think its time for us
to look at a different vendor

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.740
to provide one single
vendor lock down.

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:54.600
And plus, we have been
running in BigQuery

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:57.882
at that time for over two years.

00:27:57.882 --> 00:27:59.340
We think it just
makes sense for us

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:02.400
to put our Hadoop
and Spark technology

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:07.590
closer to our BigQuery piece.

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:09.660
And the last piece
is we were running

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:11.970
a lot of machine-learning
module at that time.

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:13.470
And we think it's
the time for us

00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:15.660
to start utilizing
some of the TPU power

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.620
that provided by
Google Cloud ML.

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:22.690
All right.

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:28.300
What specifically why do we
run elastic MapReduce and Spark

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:31.360
using GCP, not
other cloud vendors?

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:33.160
There are basically two parts.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:36.620
First of all, if you want to run
the elastic MapReduce and Spark

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:39.730
cluster, one essential
point is the cloud

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:43.450
should be able to provision
the VMs fast enough

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:49.750
to minimize the overhead of
scale up and scaling down.

00:28:49.750 --> 00:28:53.390
Google always has been known as
the new kid around the block.

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:57.890
So a lot of the APIs they can
design from the ground up.

00:28:57.890 --> 00:28:59.740
It's actually very
efficient when it

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:02.650
comes to the VM launching time.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:04.570
The other reason is the pricing.

00:29:04.570 --> 00:29:09.880
If you want to run a good,
stable, Spot Fleeting AWS,

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:15.730
you should have at least beat
around 30% on their asking

00:29:15.730 --> 00:29:16.670
price.

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:18.310
But if you want to
do the same, preempt

00:29:18.310 --> 00:29:19.810
both instances in
Google, they just

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:23.410
give you 80% straight away.

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:25.570
That's something we
consider as pricing

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:27.690
advantages over other piece.

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:34.610
How about this
specific elastic usage?

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:38.570
So this is a CPU utilization
chart I put it out

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:41.220
from about 24 hours a day.

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.040
And if we zoom in
you can actually

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:46.250
see between different
times, we're

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:49.310
just utilizing different
levels of CPU usages.

00:29:49.310 --> 00:29:53.510
And the framework itself
is about detecting what's

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:56.690
the resources needed in the
queue and trying to scale up

00:29:56.690 --> 00:29:58.980
and scaling down accordingly.

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:02.830
So you guys probably want
to argue there are just

00:30:02.830 --> 00:30:04.884
a lot of different vendors
on the market trying

00:30:04.884 --> 00:30:06.050
to do the same thing, right?

00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:10.160
There's Databricks,
there is AWS EMR,

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:14.760
and there is Kubo, all
trying to do the same thing.

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.840
Why do we have to try
to do it by ourselves

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.240
as an in-house solution?

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:23.720
The reason is we
are looking at some

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:26.660
of the specific features
is not available from one

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:27.860
single vendor.

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:29.600
For example, we
wanted cluster to be

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:33.140
able to run both Hadoop and
Spark within the same cluster.

00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:37.010
We wanted to be able to do
with Zun balancing and seeking

00:30:37.010 --> 00:30:40.790
the best pricing within
each of the Zun and capacity

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:42.440
within each of the Zun.

00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:45.410
And also we want to run
heterogeneous clusters.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:48.980
Different note sizes,
different note types, and there

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:52.190
are just other benefits, we
try to do some customizations.

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:54.470
All of these functionalities
that we require

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:57.470
didn't really come from one
single vendor at that time.

00:30:57.470 --> 00:31:01.290
So that's why we decide
to build it by ourselves.

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:05.150
And more importantly, you
know how hard can it be?

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:07.540
Turns out it's
actually not very hard.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:11.000
It actually takes our one
developer three months

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:13.040
to put all of these
pieces together.

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:15.920
So technically it's
totally doable.

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:19.100
But you know if you want to
run the actual cloud production

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:21.830
environment, the tuning
and configurations actually

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:23.450
takes longer time.

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:27.530
So first thing we did is
to separate the compute

00:31:27.530 --> 00:31:28.460
and storage.

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:31.280
That means our Hadoop and
Spark cluster doesn't really

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:34.820
persist any permanent
data ordinary HDFS files

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:36.380
within the cluster itself.

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:38.840
Instead, we persist
our permanent data

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:40.160
on the cloud storage.

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:42.590
That doesn't really mean
you cannot store any data

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:43.640
on your local hard drive.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:47.540
You can still store
temporary data and provision

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:51.050
the local hard drive for your
Hadoop and Spark cluster.

00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:54.890
The next piece will be the
MapReduce and Spark cluster

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:56.840
themselves doing
all the hard work.

00:31:56.840 --> 00:31:59.480
We're using YARN as our
resource manage layer,

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:03.710
and we use Ambari as the open
source cluster management tool.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:08.060
The last piece in there is
just a general cloud API

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:13.550
that provided Google for
spin up a VM shut down VM.

00:32:13.550 --> 00:32:19.850
So the problem is that piece and
this piece, traditionally, they

00:32:19.850 --> 00:32:21.960
don't talk to each other.

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:25.280
So all the framework's
trying to resolve

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:30.720
is talking to all of
these different pieces

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:35.070
and making sure they
work in the right order.

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:38.510
So let's look into
different pieces one by one.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:41.870
I already talked about cloud
storage PC, absolutely need

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:44.060
to separate your storage
and computing in order

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:48.410
for them to scale accordingly.

00:32:48.410 --> 00:32:51.420
And there are just two ways to
structure your Hadoop and Spark

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:52.700
cluster.

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:58.970
One way is you can have your
per cluster per job which

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:01.970
means whenever you spin up a
job, you spin up a process,

00:33:01.970 --> 00:33:03.500
you can spin up a cluster.

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:06.920
And as soon as the job finishes,
you shut down that cluster.

00:33:06.920 --> 00:33:10.260
This is actually a very
clean, easy way of doing this.

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:15.320
But we actually see a lot of
overspending and over-provision

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:16.460
happens around there.

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:18.740
For example, one of
my data scientists

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:23.000
just requires a 100-node
Hadoop cluster living in there

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:24.650
for like 10 hours.

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:25.520
Nobody knows.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:29.090
So in other words, don't
trust your user, right?

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:32.420
The way how we provision
our cluster is we

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:35.000
have a finite
number of clusters.

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:38.430
And within each cluster, with
structured it into two layers.

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:40.490
One is what we called
Persistent Node, which

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:44.480
is the node runs 2007,
which runs the Master,

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:47.070
to Namenode, Resourcemanagers
and Zookeeper,

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:50.400
et cetera, et cetera, those
controlling functions.

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:54.500
And we also have two to three
what we called Base Slave Node.

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:59.330
The reason we have the Base
Slave Node in there to run 24/7

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:03.320
is basically because a lot of
the small jobs in our system

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:04.960
comes and goes
really frequently.

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:09.080
You absolutely want to avoid
that VM launching overhead.

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:11.570
And plus, certain
may produce jobs

00:34:11.570 --> 00:34:16.100
required to persist
a application binary

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:17.820
within a cluster themselves.

00:34:17.820 --> 00:34:22.020
So those nodes are for
those purposes as well.

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:24.270
The rest of the layer will
be just the Ephemeral Layer

00:34:24.270 --> 00:34:26.389
where we're using
spot or preemptible

00:34:26.389 --> 00:34:32.330
instances so they can scale
up and down automatically.

00:34:32.330 --> 00:34:34.290
The YARN piece, there's
really just nothing

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:35.420
too much to talk about.

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:37.820
You can use Mesos
if you want to,

00:34:37.820 --> 00:34:39.980
doesn't really make a
whole lot of differences.

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:42.409
But the only thing
I want to point out

00:34:42.409 --> 00:34:44.929
is this planning
resources section,

00:34:44.929 --> 00:34:47.310
when you query that
YARN JMX matrix,

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:50.070
this is the
cluster-wide average.

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:53.420
Remember, I said our cluster
requires heterogeneous clusters

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:55.490
to run different node
sizes, different node

00:34:55.490 --> 00:34:58.160
type, different node types,
which means we can support

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:00.630
different size of containers.

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:03.830
So that means when
we run this, we're

00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:06.650
going to be a little
bit clever when

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:09.260
it comes to how much resources
is pending in the queue.

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:12.650
Because this is actually close
to average not per application

00:35:12.650 --> 00:35:15.220
or process average.

00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:16.190
So moving on.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:18.070
The next two parts
are really simple.

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:21.230
Ambari open source
Cluster Management Layer

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:24.140
defines what components
install on a cluster.

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:27.320
Within each of the components,
what other configurations are.

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:30.320
This is actually optional piece.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.841
I'm not sure if we have any
Hortonworks people sitting

00:35:33.841 --> 00:35:34.340
here.

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:38.590
But if your workload is simple,
if your cluster component-wise

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:40.620
is simple, you can
ignore this piece.

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:45.260
But this is a good tool,
open source from Hortonworks.

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:49.020
In our cases, we just grab it
and use it in our framework.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:50.790
The next one is cloud API.

00:35:50.790 --> 00:35:53.880
There's just not really
too much to talk about it.

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:55.580
But one thing I
want to point out

00:35:55.580 --> 00:36:03.680
is try to extract your
calls to the VM layer.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:08.710
Let's say if I want to
support multi-cloud operations

00:36:08.710 --> 00:36:10.040
in my clusters--

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:11.430
Google people may
not like this--

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:14.060
but let's say you want to
support both the AWS and Google

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:15.990
within my framework--

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:18.770
when my framework
wants to span up a VM,

00:36:18.770 --> 00:36:20.600
the underlying layer
needs to be abstracted

00:36:20.600 --> 00:36:23.630
to know how to span
up a VM in Google

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:28.430
as well as how to
open up a VM in AWS.

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:30.440
The next one is
just a quick example

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:34.640
of what they called blueprint
definition from Ambari which

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:39.080
gives you the definitions in
a JSON format for Host Group

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:41.220
components definition,
other configurations,

00:36:41.220 --> 00:36:43.370
and hosting classifications.

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:45.530
And at runtime,
Ambari can just grab

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:49.610
all of these configurations
and construct a cluster

00:36:49.610 --> 00:36:50.763
by themselves.

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:59.210
When a resource request
coming into the queue,

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:01.040
those are the life cycles.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:02.927
Very intuitive.

00:37:02.927 --> 00:37:04.885
When there is a request,
we check whether there

00:37:04.885 --> 00:37:06.570
are resources available.

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:10.200
And then we run a job
after job is done.

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.410
We shut down the node.

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:12.630
Very intuitive.

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:15.670
What's the hard part
on this diagram?

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:21.560
The hard part is
actually this piece.

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:24.350
When do you try
to release a node?

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:25.830
That comes to two parts.

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:32.450
One is how do you actively
detect which node is idle?

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:35.740
And the second one is how do
you deal with sudden termination

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.370
of your node?

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:41.470
Let's say in the middle of a
job, one of my underlying node

00:37:41.470 --> 00:37:44.510
just get kicked out from
the cloud themselves

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:50.210
because of supply constraints
on the preemptible instances.

00:37:50.210 --> 00:37:54.620
So I'm going to touch those two
pieces in the later on slides.

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:58.370
This is the framework
general structure.

00:37:58.370 --> 00:38:00.410
I'm not going to go into
too much into details,

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:02.570
you can always refer
back to the slides.

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:04.550
The only thing that
I want to call out

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:06.620
is this particular piece.

00:38:06.620 --> 00:38:08.930
When you run a heterogeneous
cluster of different node

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:11.690
sizes, different node
types, different node types,

00:38:11.690 --> 00:38:15.560
you absolutely want to normalize
each of these node types

00:38:15.560 --> 00:38:18.140
and resources into a
single unit so you can

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:21.500
do across the board comparison.

00:38:21.500 --> 00:38:26.690
For example, if you have
a X large instances,

00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:29.540
and if you have n4
xlarge instances-- when

00:38:29.540 --> 00:38:33.440
it comes to computational
resources and job resources,

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:35.180
how do you compare
these two together?

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:37.880
In our cases, we normalize
into one single base

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:41.990
unit, the r4
equivalent 1.5 SCUs,

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:44.990
the n4 equivalent to 1.2 SCUs.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:48.170
So we actually borrowed this
concept from Databricks.

00:38:48.170 --> 00:38:50.030
The way how they use
this normalization

00:38:50.030 --> 00:38:52.880
is to charge pricing
for their customer.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:55.100
What we think is,
that's a cool concept

00:38:55.100 --> 00:39:00.050
we can use it to compare
resources requirements as well.

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:05.000
And those are the actual logic
when it comes to the resource

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:06.540
calculations.

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:10.580
Unfortunately, Tapjoy is
not an open source company,

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:14.150
so I'm not actually able to
show all of our actual code here

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:19.130
but I use pseudo-code
to describe the logic.

00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:21.740
You also probably notice I
stripped out a price beating

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:22.400
part.

00:39:22.400 --> 00:39:24.560
This is solely for AWS.

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:26.200
As for Google, you
don't really need

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:29.570
to do that they just gave you
80% discount straight away.

00:39:29.570 --> 00:39:31.940
The only reason we
want to do this in AWS.

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:34.730
It's because we
train our own module

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:38.540
to look at a
histogram of the price

00:39:38.540 --> 00:39:41.450
as well as all the
spikes on the pricing

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:44.570
to try to predict all
the pricing at a given

00:39:44.570 --> 00:39:49.190
future for a particularly
instant type for a given Zun.

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:52.740
But that case doesn't really
apply to the GCE part.

00:39:55.530 --> 00:39:57.990
The next few slides
it's about how

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:01.446
do you tune your cluster
on the cloud environment?

00:40:01.446 --> 00:40:02.820
There are a lot
of configurations

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:06.090
and considerations that
we learn from running

00:40:06.090 --> 00:40:09.990
our elastic MapReduce
on the cloud.

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:12.750
But I'm not going to go
through into the details,

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:15.390
the only thing I
want to call out

00:40:15.390 --> 00:40:17.910
is this specific
piece on the top.

00:40:17.910 --> 00:40:21.620
How do we define idle
resources within your cluster?

00:40:25.530 --> 00:40:28.880
It's actually working different
ways in Spark and Hadoop.

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:30.960
In Spark, executor
doesn't really

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:34.110
exit until this job finishes.

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:36.090
So it's actually
very clean and neat.

00:40:36.090 --> 00:40:41.520
You can shut down a cluster
resources if there is the Spark

00:40:41.520 --> 00:40:44.800
or a particular Spark
job is finished.

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:47.060
However, when it
comes to MapReduce,

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:51.650
a release on the computational
resource on Hadoop

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:55.640
doesn't really mean to release
of the entire resources

00:40:55.640 --> 00:40:57.790
for that MapReduce job.

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:59.430
For example, if
you have a mapper

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:01.740
produce some intermediate
output and store

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:06.780
on a local hard drive,
although the mapper finishes

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:08.910
and release the
computational resource,

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:14.230
it doesn't release the local
temporary data that generated,

00:41:14.230 --> 00:41:17.400
which is going to be utilizing
in the later on stage

00:41:17.400 --> 00:41:19.960
after shuffling in
the reducing stages.

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:23.790
So if you chop down that note
at this particular moment,

00:41:23.790 --> 00:41:26.460
you reduce or will have to
go asking the mapper again

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:28.950
that mapper is going
to span up once again

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:31.080
to reproduce those data.

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:33.580
So you got to be careful
not only checking

00:41:33.580 --> 00:41:37.600
the computational research
released from a MapReduce job

00:41:37.600 --> 00:41:41.910
but also check whether
the data are also released

00:41:41.910 --> 00:41:44.700
from that particular node.

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:47.190
All right so for the
last few minutes,

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:49.860
I absolutely want
to attach the point,

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:55.170
how to manage instance lifecycle
on a preemptible instances

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:58.680
fleet within GCP.

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:02.370
So this is about
two weeks of data

00:42:02.370 --> 00:42:06.960
I took from the end of 2017.

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:10.530
Each of the bars here
shows you how long

00:42:10.530 --> 00:42:16.080
each preemptive instances runs
before the cloud kicked them

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:16.740
out.

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:18.810
So we're not trying to
proactively kick them out.

00:42:18.810 --> 00:42:21.840
We simply launch the instances,
have them sitting in there

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:24.660
and try to see how long it goes.

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:26.740
The scales are a little
bit difficult to see,

00:42:26.740 --> 00:42:30.870
but the right line here
marks about one hour.

00:42:30.870 --> 00:42:32.430
And if we zoom in,
we can actually

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:35.210
see the lifecycle of
a preemptible instance

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:37.950
within Google cloud
are very sparse.

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:41.780
So within a framework, not
only just MapReduce or Spark

00:42:41.780 --> 00:42:43.280
or whatever the
service layer you'll

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:45.810
run with on a cloud
environment, if you

00:42:45.810 --> 00:42:50.190
want to utilize a large amount
of preemptive instances,

00:42:50.190 --> 00:42:53.460
like in our case we
run 99% of our workload

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:56.250
on preemptible instances
for Hadoop and Spark,

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:59.040
you've got to have some
fall over strategies

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:03.240
to protect yourself, to
ease the DevOps operations.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:09.000
Fortunately, YARN
already handles three out

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:11.370
of the four pieces free.

00:43:11.370 --> 00:43:13.850
But you still need to do
something for yourself.

00:43:13.850 --> 00:43:16.780
If you're on your job pipeline
level, if you want to--

00:43:16.780 --> 00:43:19.920
let's say if you're running
in Jenkins and or Airflow,

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:24.420
always have a retry strategy so
that your DevOps people don't

00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:26.400
need to go in there
and rerun the job

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:29.430
if they've ever failed because
of the preemptive instance got

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:30.660
take away.

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:35.430
But even if the above is
not very safe as well--

00:43:35.430 --> 00:43:38.170
let's say, in our cases,
by the end of the day,

00:43:38.170 --> 00:43:41.550
we try to run an end-of-day
batch to refresh our modules

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:46.440
and refresh all the statistics
we used to build up the module.

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:48.600
All of these jobs
runs really long,

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:49.740
about three or four hours.

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:53.520
Inevitably, all of
the retry has a limit.

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:58.440
So the way how we do
this is, first of all,

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:00.420
at the beginning
of this batch, we

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:03.420
washed out all the
preemptible instances.

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:04.940
And then we filled
up the cluster

00:44:04.940 --> 00:44:07.230
with non-preemptible ones.

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:09.420
We hold this
cluster size steady.

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:13.220
By the end of the
batch, we washed out

00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:15.750
all of the non-preemptible
ones and we

00:44:15.750 --> 00:44:21.060
let the cluster do themselves,
sorry do their own things using

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:24.450
preemptible instances.

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:28.110
This is actually a
very clean and neat way

00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:30.222
of doing this,
guaranteed to work.

00:44:30.222 --> 00:44:31.680
The downside of
this is when you're

00:44:31.680 --> 00:44:33.840
used to non-preemptible
instances

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:38.040
you got to pay like eight
times more expensive

00:44:38.040 --> 00:44:41.430
than the preemptible ones.

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:44.610
At the beginning of
our adoption into GCP

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:46.350
this is actually the
way in production,

00:44:46.350 --> 00:44:48.250
but these days it's not.

00:44:48.250 --> 00:44:50.310
These days we do a hack.

00:44:50.310 --> 00:44:52.920
If you're interested
how do we do

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:54.630
that for preemptible
instances, feel

00:44:54.630 --> 00:44:58.620
free to contact me
because that hack relies

00:44:58.620 --> 00:45:01.600
on analyzing Google's
behavior of its own data.

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:04.890
So I don't think I should be
able to disclose on the public.

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:08.160
But ping me if you have
that question in mind

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:09.660
after the work.

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:11.250
I know there's a
lot of, again, I

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:13.820
know there's a lot of
materials I went through.

00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:15.990
For the specific logic
and configurations,

00:45:15.990 --> 00:45:18.300
refer back to my
slides if you want to.

